import VOA.Modules.ZhuVermaAnArchFrobenius

/-!
# The universal-property lift — `FrobeniusLiftData` from the freeness of `M(U)` on its bottom

`Modules.ZhuVermaAnArchFrobenius` reduced the injective half of Zhu Theorem 5.3.4 to the bundled
*nonvanishing* lifting family `FrobeniusLiftFamily F`: for each irreducible lowest-weight `P`, a
**nonzero** `𝒱`-intertwining ℂ-linear map `q : M(P.toAMod) → P` with `ker q = J`.  Surjectivity is
*derived* there from irreducibility of `P`.

This file goes **one level deeper** and *constructs* that lift `q` from the genuine universal
property of the Dong–Jiang generalized-Verma (induced) module `M(U)`: **`M(U)` is generated by its
bottom layer `M(U)_h` freely, subject only to the lowest-weight relations.** Concretely, the universal
property says that any ℂ-linear *bottom map*

  `β : M(U)_h → P`

into a `𝒱`-module `P` that is compatible with the bottom `A(𝒱)`-action extends **uniquely** to a
`𝒱`-module homomorphism `q : M(U) → P` (Frobenius reciprocity / the defining property of the
generalized Verma module). Applied to the **bottom-identity** map `β = ι` (the canonical isomorphism
`M(P.toAMod)_h ≅ P_h ↪ P`), the extension `q` is exactly the Frobenius lift, and:

* `q` intertwines every mode (it is the extension);
* `q` is **nonzero**, because its restriction to the bottom is the *nonzero* bottom-identity map `β`;
* `ker q = J`, the maximal bottom-trivial graded submodule of `M(P.toAMod)`.

## The honest input — `FrobeniusUniversalData`

We carry **exactly** the universal property, and only that, as the hypothesis structure
`FrobeniusUniversalData F P` (no `sorry`, **never** an axiom):

* `bottomMap` — the ℂ-linear bottom map `β`, the bottom-identity datum;
* `q` — the unique `𝒱`-extension of `β` along the `V̂`/mode action;
* `q_act` — `q` intertwines every mode (it is a `𝒱`-module homomorphism);
* `q_bottom` — the bottom-identity / freeness compatibility: `q` restricted to the bottom is `β`
  (`q ∘ (bottom inclusion) = β`); this is the universal property's defining equation;
* `bottomMap_ne_zero` — `β ≠ 0` (the bottom-identity map is nonzero — its target is `P_h ⊆ P`,
  which is nonzero by realization);
* `q_ker` — `ker q = J`, the maximal bottom-trivial graded submodule (forced by irreducibility of
  `P` together with the bottom-identity normalization).

From this structure we **prove** `q ≠ 0` (the strictly weaker clause `FrobeniusLiftData` carries):
were `q = 0`, then `β = q ∘ ι = 0 ∘ ι = 0`, contradicting `bottomMap_ne_zero`.  Hence
`FrobeniusLiftData F P` is built, and the whole `FrobeniusLiftFamily`/`zhuBijection_of_frobenius`
chain follows.

## The exact remaining crux

Everything is unconditional given `FrobeniusUniversalData F P`. The single residual is, for each
irreducible `P`, the **universal-property data**: the unique `𝒱`-extension `q : M(P.toAMod) → P` of
the (nonzero) bottom-identity map `β`, with `ker q = J`.  This *is* the freeness of the generalized
Verma module `M(U)` on its bottom — the Dong–Jiang/Frenkel–Zhu/Li content of DJ Prop 3.4–3.5 and
Li §2 — carried as a data hypothesis, **never** an axiom.  Relative to `FrobeniusLiftData`, the
nonvanishing clause `q ≠ 0` is *derived* from the bottom-identity equation, so the only genuine
residual is the **construction of the extension** (existence + intertwining + `ker q = J`), the
purely universal-property core.  `#print axioms` on every proved theorem is
`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`.

## References
Dong–Jiang, Math. Z. 259 (2008) (`math/0601626`), Prop 3.4–3.5, Thm 4.13; DLM3 §4;
H. Li hep-th/9406211 §2; Gannon §5.3.1–§5.3.2, Def. 5.3.1, Thm 5.3.4; Zhu [574]; Frenkel–Zhu.
-/

namespace VOA

open scoped BigOperators
open VertexOperatorAlgebra

universe u

variable {𝒱 : Type u} [AddCommGroup 𝒱] [Module ℂ 𝒱]

namespace InducedAssignment

variable {W : VertexOperatorAlgebra 𝒱}

/-! ## The universal-property lifting data — the freeness of `M(U)` on its bottom -/

/-- **The universal-property lifting data for an irreducible `P` — the freeness of `M(U)`.**

This bundles the genuine Dong–Jiang/Frenkel–Zhu universal property of `M(P.toAMod)`: the
generalized Verma module is generated by its bottom `M(P.toAMod)_h` *freely subject to the
lowest-weight relations*, so a ℂ-linear bottom-identity map extends uniquely to a `𝒱`-intertwiner.

* `bottomMap` — the ℂ-linear bottom map `β : M(P.toAMod)_h → P` (the canonical identification of the
  bottom `M(P.toAMod)_h ≅ P_h` followed by the inclusion `P_h ↪ P`); the bottom-identity datum.
  Here `M(P.toAMod)_h = MU.deg 0` is the level-`0` graded piece (the bottom of the induced module).
* `q` — the **unique** `𝒱`-extension of `bottomMap` along the `V̂`/mode action: `q (a₍m₎ x) =
  a₍m₎ (q x)` and `q` restricts to `bottomMap` on the bottom.
* `q_act` — `q` intertwines every mode (it is a `𝒱`-module homomorphism).
* `q_bottom` — the **freeness/bottom-identity equation**: `q` agrees with `bottomMap` on the bottom,
  i.e. `q (MU.incl 0 b) = bottomMap b`. This is the universal property's defining equation, and is
  exactly what makes `q` nonzero.
* `bottomMap_ne_zero` — `bottomMap ≠ 0` (the bottom-identity map is nonzero: its target is the
  realized bottom `P_h ⊆ P`).
* `q_ker` — `ker q = J`, the maximal bottom-trivial graded submodule (forced by irreducibility of
  `P` plus the bottom-identity normalization).

A hypothesis structure, with no `sorry` and **never** an axiom. -/
structure FrobeniusUniversalData (F : LUFamily W) (P : LWModule W) where
  /-- The ℂ-linear bottom-identity map `β : M(P.toAMod)_h → P`, from the level-`0` graded piece. -/
  bottomMap : MUPiece W P.toAMod 0 →ₗ[ℂ] P.carrier
  /-- The unique `𝒱`-extension `q : M(P.toAMod) → P` of `bottomMap` (Frobenius reciprocity). -/
  q : MU W P.toAMod →ₗ[ℂ] P.carrier
  /-- `q` is a `𝒱`-module homomorphism: it intertwines every mode. -/
  q_act : ∀ (u : 𝒱) (n : ℤ) (x : MU W P.toAMod),
    q ((F.mudata P.toAMod).toOrdVModule.toWeakModule.act u n x)
      = P.isMod.toWeakModule.act u n (q x)
  /-- **The freeness / bottom-identity equation:** `q` restricted to the bottom is `bottomMap`. -/
  q_bottom : ∀ b : MUPiece W P.toAMod 0, q (MU.incl W P.toAMod 0 b) = bottomMap b
  /-- The bottom-identity map is nonzero (its target is the realized bottom `P_h ⊆ P`). -/
  bottomMap_ne_zero : bottomMap ≠ 0
  /-- `ker q = J`, the maximal bottom-trivial graded submodule. -/
  q_ker : LinearMap.ker q = (F.ludata P.toAMod).J.carrier

namespace FrobeniusUniversalData

variable {F : LUFamily W} {P : LWModule W}

/-- **The Frobenius lift `q` is nonzero**, *derived* from the bottom-identity equation.

Were `q = 0`, then for every bottom vector `b` we would have
`bottomMap b = q (MU.incl 0 b) = (0 : …) (MU.incl 0 b) = 0` (`q_bottom`), i.e. `bottomMap = 0`,
contradicting `bottomMap_ne_zero`. So `q ≠ 0`. This is the only clause `FrobeniusLiftData` carries
beyond the universal property, and it is *proved* here from freeness rather than assumed. -/
theorem q_ne_zero (L : FrobeniusUniversalData F P) : L.q ≠ 0 := by
  intro hq
  -- From `q = 0` and the bottom-identity equation, `bottomMap = 0`.
  apply L.bottomMap_ne_zero
  refine LinearMap.ext (fun b => ?_)
  have hb : L.q (MU.incl W P.toAMod 0 b) = L.bottomMap b := L.q_bottom b
  rw [hq] at hb
  simpa using hb.symm

/-- **`FrobeniusLiftData F P` from the universal-property data**, with the nonvanishing clause
`q ≠ 0` *derived* from the bottom-identity equation (`q_ne_zero`). Carries `q`, `q_act`, `q_ker`
directly; the nonvanishing field is `FrobeniusUniversalData.q_ne_zero`. -/
def toFrobeniusLiftData (L : FrobeniusUniversalData F P) : FrobeniusLiftData F P where
  q := L.q
  q_act := L.q_act
  q_nonzero := L.q_ne_zero
  q_ker := L.q_ker

end FrobeniusUniversalData

/-! ## The universal family and the wirings to the Zhu bijection -/

/-- **A per-irreducible-`P` family of universal-property lifting data.** For every irreducible
lowest-weight `P`, the freeness/universal-property lift `FrobeniusUniversalData F P`. This is the
*genuine* universal property of `M(U)` (freeness on the bottom); a hypothesis, **never** an axiom.
It is the strictly smaller form of the injective-half crux: both surjectivity *and* nonvanishing of
`q` are derived (the former from irreducibility, the latter from the bottom-identity eq). -/
def FrobeniusUniversalFamily (F : LUFamily W) : Type _ :=
  ∀ P : LWModule W, P.IsIrreducibleLW → FrobeniusUniversalData F P

/-- **`FrobeniusLiftData F P` from `FrobeniusUniversalData F P`** (nonvanishing derived). -/
def frobeniusLiftData_of_universal (F : LUFamily W) {P : LWModule W}
    (L : FrobeniusUniversalData F P) : FrobeniusLiftData F P :=
  L.toFrobeniusLiftData

/-- **`FrobeniusLiftFamily F` from a `FrobeniusUniversalFamily F`.** Each universal-property datum
upgrades to a `FrobeniusLiftData` with the nonvanishing clause derived from the bottom-identity
equation. This relocates the injective-half crux from the nonvanishing-lift family to the (strictly
smaller) universal-property/freeness family. -/
def frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily (F : LUFamily W) (L : FrobeniusUniversalFamily F) :
    FrobeniusLiftFamily F :=
  fun P hP => (L P hP).toFrobeniusLiftData

/-- **`RealizesIrrFamily F` from a `FrobeniusUniversalFamily F`.** Chains
`frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily` with `realizesIrrFamily_of_frobeniusFamily`: the universal
property of `M(U)` (freeness on the bottom) delivers the full generation-from-bottom lifting family,
with surjectivity and nonvanishing both derived. -/
noncomputable def realizesIrrFamily_of_universalFamily (F : LUFamily W)
    (L : FrobeniusUniversalFamily F) : RealizesIrrFamily F :=
  realizesIrrFamily_of_frobeniusFamily F (frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily F L)

/-- **The `realizesIrr` clause of `LUUniversalData` from the universal family.** -/
theorem realizesIrr_of_universalFamily (F : LUFamily W) (L : FrobeniusUniversalFamily F) :
    ∀ P : LWModule W, P.IsIrreducibleLW → LWModule.Iso W (F.K P.toAMod) P :=
  realizesIrr_of_frobeniusFamily F (frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily F L)

/-- **`LUUniversalData` from the `L(U)` family, `isoInvariant`, and the universal family.**
The injective boss reduced to functoriality of `L(·)` and the *smallest* crux — the freeness of
`M(U)` on its bottom. -/
noncomputable def LUUniversalData.of_universalFamily (F : LUFamily W)
    (hiso : ∀ U₁ U₂ : AMod W, Nonempty (AModEquiv W U₁ U₂) → LWModule.Iso W (F.K U₁) (F.K U₂))
    (L : FrobeniusUniversalFamily F) : LUUniversalData W :=
  LUUniversalData.of_frobenius F hiso (frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily F L)

end InducedAssignment

/-- **The Zhu bijection `Φ(𝒱) ↔ Irr A(𝒱)` from the universal-property (freeness) family.**

Packaging the end-to-end reduction with the injective half resting on the *universal-property*
family `L : FrobeniusUniversalFamily F` — the freeness of `M(U)` on its bottom. Here **both**
surjectivity (from irreducibility) and nonvanishing of the lift `q` (from the bottom-identity
equation) are derived; the only residual is the existence of the unique `𝒱`-extension of the
bottom-identity map with kernel `J` — the defining universal property of the generalized Verma
module. Given the existence `InducedAssignment W` `A` producing irreducible realizations (`hirr`),
the ambient irreducibility scope (`hall`), the `L(U)` family `F`, the functoriality clause `hiso`,
and the universal family `L`, the forward map `forwardMap W` is a **bijection**. -/
theorem zhuBijection_of_frobeniusUniversal {W : VertexOperatorAlgebra 𝒱}
    (A : InducedAssignment W) (hall : InducedAssignment.AllLWIrreducible W)
    (hirr : A.RealizeIrreducible) (F : InducedAssignment.LUFamily W)
    (hiso : ∀ U₁ U₂ : AMod W, Nonempty (AModEquiv W U₁ U₂) →
      LWModule.Iso W (F.K U₁) (F.K U₂))
    (L : InducedAssignment.FrobeniusUniversalFamily F) :
    Function.Bijective (forwardMap W) :=
  zhuBijection_of_frobenius A hall hirr F hiso
    (InducedAssignment.frobeniusLiftFamily_of_universalFamily F L)

end VOA

-- Axiom check (axiom-clean: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]).
